#! ia 1.0

@convention authoring-ia-record-syntax
  meaning
    says "IA record syntax (the rule sheet's first section). A file starts #! ia 1.0; # comment lines are admitted; a record is @word record-name, with head fields directly under it (a contract's version, a system's provider), then sections whose fields are typed by the schema: text in double quotes, id a bare word, number, list [a, b], ref @word name, refs [@word a, @word b]; relationships use the surface verbs governs, enforces, implements, cites, requires, uses, supersedes, and a fragment ref (@contract c#REQ-ID) names one requirement of a contract. Identity is <owner-system>/<kind>/<facet>/<name>, the owner being the system that owns the word, not the folder the record is authored in; a name must be unique per word across the whole workspace, so prefix every new name with the authoring system's slug. Reading a schema: ia vocabulary <word> --schema --json lists a shipped word's sections and fields, each marked required or not, with types and enums, and names its owner; it reads only the catalogue shipped with the CLI (44 words in 3.1.1, 3.2.2, 3.3.0 and 3.4.0) and needs no workspace; it takes no --root (exit 2) and refuses a word the workspace mints (IA-CLI-USAGE Unknown word); --search <text> matches words and descriptions; --example prints No example is shipped for every word in 3.1.1, 3.2.2, 3.3.0 and 3.4.0 (exit 1). For a local word, read .ia/src/systems/<owner>/schemas/<word>.schema.ia and the owner's system.ia discriminators, and find its records with ia inspect or the machine protocol's ia search. What a closed schema refuses: an unlisted section (IA-COMP-SECTION-UNKNOWN); an unlisted field inside a declared section (IA-COMP-FIELD-UNKNOWN), except under governance, where any key is admitted (R11); declared fields are type- and enum-checked (IA-COMP-FIELD-VALUE); misspelling a required field is caught because the required field is then missing (IA-LANG-CASE-MALFORMED, Scenario requires evaluator., for evaluatr, re-verified on 3.3.0 and 3.4.0); a missing required field is IA-COMP-FIELD-MISSING (@schema <word> requires '<field>', 3.3.0 and 3.4.0). Contracts hold requirements in sections such as invariants, outputs, inputs, preconditions and failures, each line REQ-ID text, and must govern something (R14). Status enums (3.3.0 and 3.4.0, language 3.2.0, unchanged from 2.0.0): plan, milestone and task proposed, open, held, closed, dropped, superseded; decision open, made, superseded, withdrawn; spec draft, accepted, superseded, withdrawn. A milestone needs work.plan and work.exit; a task needs work.milestone. Playbook cognition may hold orient, plan, act and learn blocks, each with primary <Primitive> and one or more <Primitive> means lines; the primitives are Memory, Attention, Inference, Decision, Escalation and Learning."
    answers "How is an IA record written, how is a schema read, and what does a closed schema refuse?"
  governance
    severity advisory
    requires "Write records in this syntax, read each word's shape from its schema, and prefix every new name with the authoring system's slug."
  subject
    subject-word system

@convention authoring-ia-rules-ownership
  meaning
    says "IA admission rules R1 and R2 (re-verified on 3.4.0, language 3.2.0). Re-verify against the target workspace's own toolchain before relying on a rule: languages and CLIs differ by version, and this sheet has already been wrong seven times. It first said unknown fields pass silently everywhere, but a wider probe showed that is true only under governance (R11); it said an edge needs only the target owner's consent, but IA checks the source owner too (R2); it said IA-COMP-NOT-EVALUATED comes from test evaluators, but it comes from adoptions (R10); it said ia validate refuses an unknown evaluator, which stopped being true in 3.0 (R3); it said the language refuses a decision's requires of a contract (IA-LANG-EDGE-UNCONSENTED), but 3.2.2's and 3.3.0's ia validate report that refusal as a graph one (R2); it then said only ia preview --relate gives the language code, but the 3.4.0 probe found that the code depends on the file layout, on 3.3.0 as well: with both ends in one file ia validate and ia capture give the language code too (R2); it said the @hook guard judges an edit by the mandates whose authority.covers claims the path, but on 3.3.0 and 3.4.0 it judges the acting participant's Synthesis move whatever covers says (3.2.2 was not re-checked for it). Two other facts changed after the 3.2.2 probe rather than having been wrong: language 3.2.0 gives work-system a govern row for @law (R2), and a restore across language bases now refuses with IA-DIST-CONFLICT from the default registry (the toolchain facts). The probe re-checks a rule in under a minute. R1: a folder may author a word only if its system directly requires the word's owner; transitive requires do not count; error IA-COMP-DISCRIMINATOR-FOREIGN; fix: add the owner to the system's requires. Owners: capability, agent-profile, voice, harness, execution-binding to agent-composition-system; agent, mandate to agent-system; contract, check, case to compliance-system; law, principle, convention, playbook to governance-system; plan, milestone, task, decision, spec to work-system; workspace, distribution to workspace-system; authoring-guide, operation to authoring-system; template to template-system; hook to hook-authoring-system; observation, improvement to learning-system; run to session-system. R2: an edge needs consent from both endpoint word owners, the system that mints the source record's word and the one that mints the target's; each declares consent in its system.ia edges block, rows reading <predicate> <targets> using <sources>; one row must match both ends, separate rows never combine; cite * using thing lets thing records cite anything (outgoing), cite thing using * lets anything cite thing records (incoming); IA checks the source owner first, then the target owner, and reports the side that refuses; consent is keyed by the system that mints the word, not the folder; a domain system that mints no words needs no consent rows; a system with no edges block refuses every edge to or from its words. Every other public system consents to cite, govern, enforce, implement, require and use * using * (learning-system also supersede), unchanged in language 3.2.0; work-system 3.2.0 (CLI 3.3.0 and 3.4.0) is narrower, as 1.2.0, 3.1.1 and 3.1.2 were: cite * using *; govern plan, milestone, task, decision, spec using law (new in 3.2.0); require task, milestone, decision using task, milestone, any-adopter; require contract, spec using spec; supersede like-for-like; ground plan, milestone, task, spec using decision, any-adopter; its one govern row takes only a @law source: on 3.3.0 and 3.4.0 a @law governing a @plan, @milestone, @task, @decision or @spec is admitted (ia validate, ia capture and ia preview --relate), where 3.2.2 refuses it on the target side (target system refuses govern), and a @convention governing a task is still refused on the target side; no implement, enforce or use rows (a law's enforces @task is refused on the target side, 3.3.0 and 3.4.0), and a decision cannot require a contract (source refused, 2.0.0, 3.1.1, 3.2.2, 3.3.0 and 3.4.0). Every @system belongs to IA's floor, whose consent rows admit only cite schema using *, so any edge onto a @system is refused on the target side. Error IA-GRAPH-EDGE-UNCONSENTED (source system refuses or target system refuses), on 3.2.2, 3.3.0 and 3.4.0 also for a decision's requires of a contract in another file from ia validate and ia capture, with or without #REQ; with the decision and the contract in one file ia validate and ia capture refuse it as IA-LANG-EDGE-UNCONSENTED (The source system work-system does not consent to require decision -> contract.; 3.3.0 and 3.4.0), as they do a @convention governing a task in the same file, and while such a language error stands the run reports no graph-stage refusal elsewhere; ia preview --relate refuses that edge before writing as IA-LANG-EDGE-UNCONSENTED (The source system work-system, which registers @decision, does not consent to require decision -> contract), which this sheet recorded for it on 2.0.0 and 3.1.1; warning IA-COMP-CONSENT-EMPTY (<system> has no consent row naming <word>) on the minted word of a system with no edges block (re-verified on 3.1.1, 3.2.2, 3.3.0 and 3.4.0, and 2.0.0 gave it too). Fix: target refused, point the edge at a record whose owner consents (a component, a workspace or a contract), never @system <domain system>; source refused, add the narrowest row to the source owner's edges naming the minted word on the using side (cite contract using thing), or use a predicate a framework owner admits, such as cites; a rule over work records is, on language 3.2.0, a @law that governs them (a @convention that governs them, or a law that enforces them, is still refused, and 3.2.2 refuses the law too)."
    answers "Which words may a system folder author, and which edges does IA consent to?"
  governance
    severity advisory
    requires "Require every word owner directly, draft the narrowest consent rows on both ends of every edge, and never point an edge at a @system."
  subject
    subject-word system

@convention authoring-ia-rules-checks-and-coverage
  meaning
    says "IA admission rules R3 to R6. R3: check.runs must name a built-in evaluator: COMP-PARSE, COMP-SCHEMA, COMP-SYSTEM, COMP-SCHEMA-ENROLLED, COMP-CONSENT-DECLARED, COMP-CONSENT, COMP-IDENTITY, COMP-SELECTOR, COMP-VARIANT, COMP-COVERAGE, COMP-FRAGMENT, COMP-ADOPTION, COMP-KERNEL, COMP-FIXTURES, COMP-STEWARD, COMP-BOOTSTRAP or COMP-CHECK (the same seventeen in 3.1.1, 3.2.2, 3.3.0 and 3.4.0); a trusted evaluator catalog is the only alternative; changed in 3.0: ia validate, ia preview and ia author admit a check on any other runner and bind it (unavailable without a host catalog), listing it under the validate report's checks with no finding, while ia capture, ia host, ia project, ia restore and every other reader still refuse it, so a clean ia validate does not prove this rule and the probe reads validate's check bindings to refuse such a check; a built-in check binds to the admission verdicts its check.scope selects (* for all of them, otherwise those whose scope equals it, so a prose scope stays not-evaluated); error IA-COMP-CHECK-UNKNOWN (from capture, host, project, restore and the probe); fix: use COMP-COVERAGE, which IA really evaluates for the contract, and name the behavioural test in each case's evaluator. R4: every contract requirement needs at least one unconditional implementing @case, and the whole contract needs at least one success case and at least one failure or refusal case; this is contract-wide, not per requirement (a success case on one requirement and a failure case on the other is admitted); on 2.0.0, 3.1.1, 3.2.2, 3.3.0 and 3.4.0 IA-COMP-COVERAGE-KIND was reported only once every requirement had a case; errors IA-COMP-COVERAGE-MISSING, IA-COMP-COVERAGE-KIND; fix: add the cases; per-requirement pairs are a workspace's own rule, not IA's. R5: requires @contract X#REQ is an adoption: IA tries to evaluate the clause and reports every requirement of the contract as not evaluated, even for a #REQ-scoped clause; warning IA-COMP-NOT-EVALUATED; fix: for traceability use cites @contract X#REQ, which adds no warning; use requires only when you mean adoption. R6: one local @agent steward per system, named by the @system head's steward, whose unconditioned governance.applies names every word the system mints; re-verified on 3.1.1, 3.2.2, 3.3.0 and 3.4.0: a system minting a word its steward does not apply to is refused; error IA-COMP-STEWARD-MISSING (<system> needs exactly one local @agent steward and unconditioned governance.applies coverage for <word>); fix: mint words in system.ia discriminators with one @schema each, and list them in applies."
    answers "Which check ids, case coverage, adoptions and stewards does IA admit?"
  governance
    severity advisory
    requires "Use only built-in COMP-* evaluators, give every requirement a case and the contract both kinds, cite rather than require a contract for traceability, and cover every minted word in the steward's applies."
  subject
    subject-word check

@convention authoring-ia-rules-schemas-and-ids
  meaning
    says "IA admission rules R8 and R10 to R14 (R7 and R9 described 1.x commands and are retired; their numbers are not reused). R8: ia validate renders 50 findings by default (truncated true in JSON; 3.1.1, 3.2.2, 3.3.0 and 3.4.0: 62 findings gave 50, and on 3.2.2 and 3.3.0 --severity error alone still stops at 50, and on 3.3.0 and 3.4.0 --max-findings 100 gave all 62); use --severity error to see errors only and --max-findings N for everything. R10: IA-COMP-NOT-EVALUATED warnings come from adoptions (requires @contract, R5) and from two global notices, COMP-FIXTURES and COMP-KERNEL (matching-revision verification evidence was not supplied), which ia validate --verify-conformance (new in 3.4.0) replaces with two verified passes on a workspace whose floor is the embedded one; cases do not produce them; each adopting record gets one warning for every requirement of the adopted contract, even when the clause names a single #REQ (2.0.0, 3.1.1, 3.2.2, 3.3.0 and 3.4.0: one #REQ-01 adoption of a two-requirement contract gave two, each No structural evaluator is supplied); in one workspace, 57 adopting records times 18 requirements plus 2 gave 1,028; they are warnings and do not block admission; report the count grouped by record; a new warning on a record you wrote means it adopts a contract. R11: closed schemas refuse unknown sections and fields, except under governance, whose keys are compiler-consumed clauses (the variant requires, the severity dimension) and are never field-checked, so any key there is admitted; observed on @agent, @mandate and @law in 1.1.0, re-verified on @law in 2.0.0, 3.1.1, 3.2.2, 3.3.0 and 3.4.0, where an unknown section is still refused, and since 3.1.1 an unknown meaning field on @agent is refused too; a misspelled optional clause such as require [...] validates and does nothing; head, cognition, activation, relationships and contract REQ-* lines are not field-checked either, their own grammar applies; fix: run the governance lint. R12: requirement ids are global across the workspace, not scoped to their contract; two contracts that both declare REQ-X-01 are refused (IA-LANG-REQUIREMENT-DUPLICATE; re-verified on 3.1.1, 3.2.2, 3.3.0 and 3.4.0, both declarations reported); prefix every requirement with its system's slug, REQ-<SLUG>-NN. R13: a playbook cell writes primary <Primitive> and a separate, sibling <Primitive> means line; nesting means under primary is refused (IA-LANG-CELL-MALFORMED, re-verified on 3.1.1, 3.2.2, 3.3.0 and 3.4.0); put both lines at the same indent under the phase. R14: a contract must have an outbound governs edge; found on 2.0.0 and re-verified on 3.1.1, 3.2.2, 3.3.0 and 3.4.0, a contract with requirements and cases but no governs is refused (IA-LANG-CONTRACT-UNBOUND, A contract requires an outbound governs binding); govern what it constrains: a component, a workspace or another consenting record, never a @system."
    answers "How many findings does validate show, where do not-evaluated warnings come from, and which schema, id, cell and contract rules does IA hold?"
  governance
    severity advisory
    requires "Validate with --severity error and --max-findings, lint governance keys, prefix requirement ids with the slug, keep cell lines siblings, and give every contract a governs edge."
  subject
    subject-word check

@convention authoring-ia-unchecked-by-admission
  meaning
    says "What admission does not check: ia validate admits these and a later consumer refuses them, so a clean ia validate proves nothing about them. H2: effect classes are exactly read, local-write and external-write. execution.effects is a list of ids, so admission takes any word (re-verified on 3.1.1, 3.2.2, 3.3.0 and 3.4.0: effects [author-records] on a @capability is admitted); the harness compiler refuses anything else on a harness's capabilities (compile.ts:19, effectList; not re-run on 2.0.0, 3.1.1, 3.2.2, 3.3.0 or 3.4.0, though 3.4.0's dist/compile.js in @inventarch/agent-composition-system is byte-identical to 3.3.0's and still holds it) with IA-COMPOSITION-CONFLICT Unknown or duplicate effect class. Fix: use read for work that only returns text or drafts, local-write for anything that writes files or runs a shell, and external-write only for a capability that really writes outside the workspace. H3: a check on a runner that is not built in (R3): ia validate admits and binds it, while ia capture (exit 1, the error in its snapshot), ia host and ia project (IA-CLI-CONFLICT, exit 3) and ia restore (IA-DIST-ADMISSION-FAILED carrying IA-COMP-CHECK-UNKNOWN) refuse it; name a built-in evaluator and probe the draft, which reports it. ia validate also admits a mandate's authority (participant, moves, scope, covers) without checking any write against it: IA 3.4.0 applies a mandate only to its own gated commands and, where the workspace authors a @hook over a path, through the guard ia host claude --apply registers, which judges the acting participant's Synthesis move whatever covers says; review mandates by reading the records (ia position --seat <identity> --json), not by trusting a clean validate."
    answers "What does ia validate admit that a later consumer refuses?"
  governance
    severity advisory
    requires "Use only the three effect classes, and review mandate authority by reading the records rather than trusting validation."
  subject
    subject-word capability

@convention authoring-ia-toolchain-facts
  meaning
    says "Toolchain facts for @inventarch/cli 3.4.0, observed with 3.1.1 on 2026-10-17, again with 3.2.2 on 2026-10-20, with 3.3.0 on 2026-10-22 and with 3.4.0 (beside 3.3.0) on 2026-10-26, unless a fact says otherwise. Which CLI runs: the pinned one (the toolchain convention); never the ia on the PATH; refuse a CLI below 3.x; ia doctor --json reports the running CLI's install channel and version; 3.4.0, like 3.3.0, 3.2.2 and 3.1.1, reads and captures a workspace still on the 2.0.0 base (language 1.2.0; 3.3.0 captured one of 566 records with 0 errors, and 3.4.0 the same workspace at 569 records), and it validated a 3.1.2-locked workspace as admitted; a CLI restores only the language base it ships, unless the workspace's own .ia/distributions/cache still holds the locked archive (gitignored, so a fresh checkout lacks it): 3.3.0 and 3.4.0 (language 3.2.0) refuse a workspace or kit locked to 3.1.2, and 3.2.2 one locked to 3.2.0, with IA-DIST-CONFLICT (exit 3, Registry no longer lists the locked release: inventarch/language@<version>) from the default registry https://api.inventarch.dev/registry/ (2026-10-22, and for 3.4.0 2026-10-26), and with that cache present 3.3.0 and 3.4.0 restored a 3.1.2-locked copy (exit 0); on 2026-10-20 this sheet recorded IA-DIST-ARTIFACT-UNAVAILABLE for 3.2.2 on a 3.1.1 lock and for 3.1.1 on a 3.1.2 lock; a 2.0.0 CLI refuses a 3.x lock offline with IA-DB-SOURCE-UNAVAILABLE (Installation engine compatibility differs; observed with the 3.2.2 probe on 2026-10-20, not re-verified on 3.3.0 or 3.4.0). Mandates gate the consumer commands: since 3.0 ia read and ia next (Observation), ia validate and ia preview (Verification), ia author (Synthesis), and ia capture, ia project and a workspace ia host --apply (Execution) are judged by the acting participant's @mandate (--actor, by default the workspace's steward or its one participant); a mandate that does not allow the move refuses before anything is read or written (IA-RUNTIME-MANDATE-MOVE, exit 1), as does an actor that is not an authored participant (IA-RUNTIME-MANDATE-ACTOR), each naming the ia position command that shows the governing mandate; ia position, ia inspect, ia init, ia vocabulary and the machine protocol are not gated; the workspace ia init creates grants its one participant all five moves; a mandate with moves [] is not admitted (IA-COMP-FIELD-VALUE), and the gated commands then refuse with IA-RUNTIME-REQUEST-INVALID (on 3.3.0 and 3.4.0: 'authority.moves' must name at least one kernel move when stated; an absent authority.moves restricts no move); naming an actor is a declaration, not authentication. Capture, not compile: ia capture [--preview] [--json] admits the workspace and writes .ia/work/snapshot/current.json (format ia-snapshot-1), rotating the previous one; --preview writes nothing; on admission errors it exits 1 and still writes the snapshot, with counts.errors set and every finding in diagnostics; ia compile was removed in 3.0 (IA-CLI-USAGE, exit 2), and ia capture takes no --out or --stdout (IA-CLI-USAGE Unknown option, exit 2), so no artifact can be written anywhere else; read-only checks capture a temp copy, never the live workspace, so the live capture pair is never rotated. Validate writes evidence: since 3.0 ia validate binds every @check and, when a bound check passed or failed, writes one @observation per such check to .ia/work/evidence/<authored revision>.ia (3.1.1, 3.2.2, 3.3.0 and 3.4.0: a COMP-COVERAGE check scoped * passed and wrote one); a not-evaluated check or a refused validation records nothing; run it on a copy when nothing may be written. Validating one system: ia validate <path> --severity error --json filters the findings to that path, but its counts and exit code stay whole-workspace: judge the filtered findings, not the exit code (observed on 3.1.1 and 3.2.2, and again on 3.3.0 and 3.4.0). Creating a workspace and a first system: ia init [<dir>] --apply --yes authors the @workspace, its participant @agent and a @mandate, installs the language (on 3.3.0 and 3.4.0 locked as inventarch/language ^3.2.0) and captures; --system also authors a local @system, its steward @agent and a @distribution; on a workspace already initialized, init refuses (IA-CLI-CONFLICT, exit 3) and writes nothing; 3.x refuses ia init for a directory inside the IA home with IA-DIST-PATH-UNSAFE (exit 3), the home being IA_HOME when it is set, else ~/.ia (with IA_HOME set elsewhere, a directory under ~/.ia is accepted); a further system is authored by hand, a folder and system.ia, with the user's approval; ia preview system <name> shows the starter a further system would get, but ia author refuses to write it (IA-CLI-EDIT-AUTHORITY). Preview and author: ia preview <word> [<name> <field>=<value>...] (also --relate and --set) shows what one new record, field or relationship would compile to, with the target file's digest, and writes nothing; ia author with the same form and --digest <sha256> writes exactly that; ia author refuses @workspace, @agent, @mandate, @system, @schema, @agent-profile and @hook changes for every actor (IA-CLI-EDIT-AUTHORITY: an owner makes them in source and validates), and a candidate with errors (IA-CLI-EDIT-UNCLEAN), so a system's stewardship records and its system.ia are still written by hand, by the owner (on 3.4.0 the help is unchanged from 3.3.0 and 3.2.2, and the refusals for all seven words and IA-CLI-EDIT-UNCLEAN were re-run on 3.3.0 and 3.4.0). Stewards are records: 3.1.1, 3.2.2, 3.3.0 and 3.4.0 render no per-system steward agent and register no per-system steward guard; ia host and ia project retire a guard a 1.x installation registered (both plan guard retire and remove each .claude/agents/<steward>.md it rendered; not re-verified on 3.3.0 or 3.4.0); @inventarch/compliance 3.1.1, 3.2.2, 3.3.0 and 3.4.0 ship no steward-profile.js, and 3.3.0 and 3.4.0 hold no composeSteward, so the 1.x steward composition check does not exist; what 3.x projects comes from authored records: ia project claude writes .claude/rules/ia-workspace.md and .claude/skills/ia-authoring/SKILL.md, plus .claude/agents/<name>.md for each authored @agent-profile and .claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md for each authored @playbook; an authored @hook (PreToolUse on Write, Edit or MultiEdit over its hook.paths) is registered only by ia host claude --apply, which also writes .mcp.json (the door's registration: server ia-workspace, command the absolute node path, args <IA home>/hosts/<release>/scripts/ia.mjs mcp --root <root>) and the projection; its group in .claude/settings.local.json matches Write|Edit|MultiEdit, with command the absolute path of the node that ran ia host (a version manager's per-shell node path does not outlive that shell) and args <IA home>/hosts/<release>/scripts/ia.mjs guard --root <root> --hook <identity> (timeout 10); on 3.3.0 and 3.4.0 the guard denies an edit under the hook's paths (permissionDecision deny with IA-HOOK-NOT-MANDATED, exit 0) when the acting participant's mandate does not allow Synthesis, whatever any mandate's authority.covers claims, and allows it when that mandate allows Synthesis, even where covers names other paths or is absent (five runs: covers claiming the folder without Synthesis, denied; covers claiming only another folder without Synthesis, denied, and with Synthesis, allowed; no covers with Synthesis, allowed, and without, denied), so the hook's paths choose what is guarded and the actor's moves decide, and covers plays no part; this sheet said on 3.2.2 that the guard judged the mandates whose covers claims the path and allowed an edit under a hook path no mandate covers, and whether 3.2.2 already behaved as 3.3.0 does was not re-checked; without an authored @hook nothing stops a write; ia position --seat floor/definition/system/<name> --json shows the system with its steward and the records around it, and a steward pointer there is readable, not an invocation. Position, next, inspect, doctor: ia position --seat <identity> --shape <shape> --phase <phase> [--primitive <primitive>] --json returns the body, its digest and a hostNote with revision, capturedRevision, freshness and key (and previousRevision when the capture is stale); ia next --json computes each task's verdict from prerequisites and evidence, not its self-declared status, and refuses IA-RUNTIME-NEXT-NO-PLAN when no @plan is admitted (and needs --seat <plan> when several are); ia inspect --json gives a workspace overview by system and kind; ia doctor --json reports the CLI version and channel, records and errors, projection drift once a host projection is written, and the registered host, and on 3.3.0 and 3.4.0 warns source-protection when mandates declare restrictions but no hook covers all of .ia/src; ia project <claude|codex> plans the position packet's host files and --apply writes them; ia project <host> --product plugin --out <dir> renders a plugin from a kit workspace, and ia init <dir> --from <plugin> lowers a Claude, Codex or Grok plugin into a new kit workspace (both new in 3.1.0; read in 3.2.2's, 3.3.0's and 3.4.0's help, not run here); that help also lists ia project <host> --product plugin --check [--compare <plugin-directory>], ia host cursor and ia host claude --user. Language 3.2.0 (new with 3.3.0, unchanged in 3.4.0): 3.3.0 ships inventarch/language 3.2.0 (its assets/base.json; archive 7b36e4b4), and 3.4.0 ships the same bytes; ia init locks it as ^3.2.0, and the catalogue still has the same 44 words (3.4.0's ia vocabulary --json is byte-identical to 3.3.0's); the base differs from 3.1.2 in four places: work-system's new govern plan, milestone, task, decision, spec using law row (R2); @run execution.phase is optional (3.2.2 refuses a run without it, IA-COMP-FIELD-MISSING @schema run requires 'execution.phase'); @template parameters and lines are optional (structured-v1 may omit this field or retain an empty list; 3.2.2 refuses a template with only filename with two IA-COMP-FIELD-MISSING); and the description of @task work.exit-evidence is reworded; everything else is a version bump, the public systems being 3.1.3, or 3.2.0 for authoring, session, template and work; a record that relies on one of these relaxations is refused by 3.2.2, so keep the fields where a 3.2.2 CLI must still read the workspace. ia position --primitive (new in 3.3.0): --primitive <Memory|Attention|Inference|Decision|Escalation|Learning> selects the primitive; without it the one derived from the shape applies, and hostNote.key gains primitive; an unknown value is refused (IA-RUNTIME-REQUEST-INVALID Scope key primitive must be one of ..., exit 2); the machine protocol is version 3 (@inventarch/runtime/dist/machine-protocol.js:130), which adds the optional primitive parameter to position; without it the version 2 keys and behaviour hold, and a door explicitly serving version 2 still refuses the parameter. ia kb (new in 3.3.0): ia kb check|search|read|resolve|traverse|pack reads and packages knowledge from .ia records and bound documents; kb check, kb search and kb read ran on a throwaway workspace (3.3.0 and 3.4.0), each answering in the envelope {version 1, ok true, value}, with citations ia.kb.citation.v1, and kb read gives the body with bodySha256, sourcePath, certified false and authority inert; on 3.4.0 kb traverse answered ok, and kb resolve did for a citation file inside the workspace and refused one outside it (IA-DB-PATH-UNSAFE, exit 3); kb pack was not run. The door (3.4.0): the MCP server ia host claude --apply registers (ia-mcp-door 3.4.0, protocol version 2025-11-25; host release bc787eb2dedf, where 3.3.0's was ia-mcp-door 3.3.0 and 34a4487f1803) lists the same 17 tools as 3.3.0, ia_scope, ia_context, ia_select, ia_get, ia_records, ia_resolve, ia_search, ia_traverse, ia_read, ia_next, ia_position and ia_vocabulary, plus ia_kb_check, ia_kb_search, ia_kb_read, ia_kb_resolve and ia_kb_traverse; there is no ia_kb_pack, so packing is CLI only; the CLI's dependency tree carries the 3.4.0 kb and steward-hook packages. ia validate --verify-conformance (new in 3.4.0) runs @inventarch/compliance's package-v1 profile in-process and reports COMP-KERNEL and COMP-FIXTURES with basis verified: on a starter workspace plain ia validate is admitted with reportOutcome not-evaluated and the two IA-COMP-NOT-EVALUATED notices (R10), and with the flag it is admitted with reportOutcome pass and no warnings, COMP-KERNEL passing against the embedded floor (4dc7d460) and COMP-FIXTURES against inventory 030084fc (121 codes observed); --json adds conformance (verdicts, profile, toolchain, kernel and fixtures, whose outOfProfile names the HOOK, RUNTIME, escalation, EXEC and PUBLICATION suites it does not run); the flag writes no evidence observation for the two checks; a workspace with errors still exits 1 (reportOutcome fail), its two checks passing; 3.3.0 refuses the flag (IA-CLI-USAGE Unknown option --verify-conformance, exit 2). Hash-pinned files: a workspace may pin source files by hash (for example a sources.ia and a source-inventory.json); editing a pinned file then needs a rehash; prefer new files; this is a workspace convention, not an IA rule."
    answers "How do the mandate gate, capture, validate, init, preview, author, position, next, inspect, doctor, project and kb behave in CLI 3.4.0?"
  governance
    severity advisory
    requires "Use these 3.4.0 behaviours as observed facts, judge filtered validate findings rather than its exit code, and re-check them when the CLI version changes."
  subject
    subject-word system

@convention authoring-ia-model-ownership
  meaning
    says "The IA authoring model, grounded against InventArch source f3a08f6 on 2026-10-11, toolchain facts re-checked on 2.0.0 on 2026-10-12, on 3.1.1 on 2026-10-17, on 3.2.2 on 2026-10-20, on 3.3.0 on 2026-10-22 and on 3.4.0 on 2026-10-26; its source references are citations, not a checkout to run. Boundary and ownership: a system owns registered vocabulary, canonical schemas, consent and direct requirements through an accountable local steward; structural validation requires a matching local steward and coverage of minted words, and does not authenticate writers. A workspace composes systems and declares captured source roots; participants can author instances of adopted vocabulary without creating a system for each role or world; the default initializer creates workspace, participant and mandate records, and --system adds a local system and native distribution; source placement and membership differ from vocabulary ownership. Native identity is system/kind/facet/name, the registration supplying owner and kind; workspace names, source folders and wrapper systems do not namespace instances of adopted words; same-band duplicates collide; preserve adopted IDs and define stable customer-instance names before composition. Public language: eleven public systems and 44 registered words, including two floor and fourteen taxonomy words; system-folder records can use their own words, directly required owners' words and floor words; current workspace-root authoring is a separate supported path; new words require a unique registration owner, canonical schema, consent and teaching guidance; a plugin component kind or document genre is not automatically a native word; relationships and typed reference fields have distinct consumers; both sides' registered owners must consent to resolved graph edges; native schema and graph checks do not qualify external implementation behaviour. Participants and authority: agent names a participant; mandate authority can declare participant, the five closed moves (Observation, Execution, Delegation, Synthesis, Verification), workspace scope, excluded words and path claims; moves are restrictions and declarations, not grants; since 3.0 the CLI consults mandates for its consumer commands: ia read and ia next (Observation), ia validate and ia preview (Verification), ia author (Synthesis), ia capture, ia project and a workspace ia host --apply (Execution) are judged by the acting participant's mandate profile (--actor, by default the workspace's steward or its one participant) and refused with IA-RUNTIME-MANDATE-MOVE or IA-RUNTIME-MANDATE-ACTOR, while ia position, ia inspect and ia init are not gated; naming an actor is a declaration, not authentication; the position packet delivers authored participant and scoped-mandate metadata, and body selection is not a participant-authentication gate; project policy, host permissions and task authorization remain separate. One steward remains an accountable system role, the agent a system names; 3.4.0, like 3.1.1, 3.2.2 and 3.3.0, renders no per-system steward agents and registers no per-system steward guard, and ia host and ia project retire a 1.x guard (observed on 3.1.1 and 3.2.2); what it projects comes from authored records (an agent file per authored agent-profile, a skill per authored playbook, and, through ia host claude --apply, a guard per authored hook that on 3.3.0 and 3.4.0 judges an edit under its paths by whether the acting participant's mandate allows Synthesis, whatever any mandate's authority.covers claims, which was not re-checked on 3.2.2), so a system folder is protected only where the workspace authors a hook over it and the acting participant's mandate withholds Synthesis."
    answers "What does IA's model say about ownership, the public language and participant authority?"
  governance
    severity advisory
    requires "Treat ownership, consent and mandates as this model states them, and re-ground any row a design relies on against the published package."
  subject
    subject-word system

@convention authoring-ia-model-delivery
  meaning
    says "The IA authoring model, continued. Composition and execution: capability groups methods, operations and checks; agent-profile selects participant, mandate, capabilities, voice and delegates; harness binds roles, workspace and execution bindings; the generic compiler checks inputs, target kinds, cycles, limits, effects and installed descriptors; native text does not install code or grant effects. Operation, model, evaluator and entry identifiers need host implementations; role briefs are not registered permissions; host multi-agent execution is distinct from declarations; independent author, critic and verifier roles are a workflow policy when needed, not a universal requirement for every trivial instance edit. Evidence and work delivery: contract, check and case declarations provide requirements and declared coverage; COMP-COVERAGE does not execute behaviour; results are pass, fail or not-evaluated; structural admission never promotes unavailable evaluator evidence; since 3.0 ia validate binds every check (a built-in evaluator to the verdicts its check.scope selects, any other runner unavailable without a host catalog) and writes a passing or failing bound check as a runtime observation under .ia/work/evidence. The next consumer computes evidenced, unblocked and blocked delivery from prerequisite relationships and success observations at the current subject digest, and does not equate self-declared task status with the verdict (packages/runtime/src/next.ts, apps/cli/src/next.ts). law, principle, convention and playbook content is delivered guidance until a qualified consumer evaluates it; observation records retain source and evidence scope; improvement is a proposed change to durable guidance; a run record does not launch work. Native release, kit and plugin: distribution describes a selected native record closure; the current packer requires release roots in native system folders and includes whole selected systems, with adopted dependencies explicitly externalized (apps/distribution/src/snapshot.ts:353). A kit is a reusable adoption offering or design profile; no kit word is registered; the historical one-workspace-root convention can be selected for a native release, not a universal plugin rule; reusable vocabulary and presets can deliberately travel, adopter-specific instances usually remain local. A plugin bundles methods, references, scripts, examples and role briefs; its package need not match a system or workspace one-for-one; native installation does not install executable implementations, and bundled helper execution is a separate action. Public framework definitions can be installed locally; hosted methods may instead return bounded construction outputs while keeping their recipes and source on the service; hidden registrations cannot be validated by a local public schema set."
    answers "What does IA's model say about composition, evidence, work delivery, distributions, kits and plugins?"
  governance
    severity advisory
    requires "Never read a declaration as running code or granted effects, and never let structural admission stand in for evaluated evidence."
  subject
    subject-word distribution

@convention authoring-ia-model-sources
  meaning
    says "The IA authoring model, sources and toolchain. Source selection and bodies: .ia/workspace.json version 1 adopted bindings name id, a canonical workspace-relative directory outside .ia, and an exact revision; its .ia/src is loaded once, without recursive imported closure; pins are computed by the framework's canonical serialized source list, so never substitute a guessed directory hash (packages/db/src/inputs.ts:332,403,412). composition.sources uses entries such as .ia/src @authored; the schema admits text while the membership consumer interprets the dialect; copy each declared or pinned source tree at its relative path in scratch probes, and keep bytes and pins unchanged. spec work.source is a document locator, not a body digest or disclosure grant; native graph revision and body SHA-256 differ; authorised body resources need their own scope and freshness checks; use the Knowledge lane's document-binding method (the public ia-kb kit's kb-bind-document) for document bodies. Toolchain: position, read and next use live admitted records; capture writes ia-snapshot-1 to .ia/work/snapshot/current.json; compile was removed in 3.0, and capture takes no --out or --stdout; do not rotate the live capture pair during a read-only survey. A scratch helper copies .ia and explicit source roots to temporary storage, captures the copy and accepts only known artifact formats; it does not upgrade dependencies or claim arbitrary JSON is an admitted corpus. Exact package and release qualification and runtime permission enforcement remain outside this model."
    answers "How are source roots selected and copied for a probe, and what do the read commands use?"
  governance
    severity advisory
    requires "Copy every declared or pinned source tree unchanged into a scratch probe, and never rotate the live capture pair during a read-only task."
  subject
    subject-word spec

@convention authoring-probe-by-hand
  meaning
    says "The probe, and how to probe without it. The probe, <kit>/scripts/probe.mjs (runnable from the kit through the core's scratch-capture helper), checks its arguments first (exit 2 on any usage error, before anything is copied), copies the workspace's .ia to a temp folder (skipping only <root>/.ia/work), drops probe files into <system>/probes/ of the copy (--system <existing system> --file <probe.ia>, with --requires adding owners to that system's requires and --set NAME=value filling placeholders) or adds each drafted system whole as .ia/src/systems/<folder name>/ (--add-system, which must not exist yet), runs IA's validation, and with --lint also captures the copy and reports governance clauses IA would admit but never use as refusals; --json gives one verdict per file; it only reads the workspace and deletes the copy unless --keep. Exit 0: every probe file admitted, and lint-clean with --lint; 1: at least one refused, or a misspelled governance key with --lint; 2: usage; 3: tool or environment failure (no CLI resolved, a CLI below 3.x, a mandate refusing the default actor, validate output unparseable, capture crashed), so the verdict is unknown, not refused, and with --json it prints status error. Relative paths resolve against the current folder, so give each path in full; probe every draft in one run so cross-references resolve; the probe takes one --system per run; never pass a drafted system.ia for an existing system with --file, which adds a second @system. Where the probe cannot run (no shell for the agent, no node), probe by hand the same way: 1, make a new empty folder in the OS temp directory (never under the workspace, and outside ~/.ia, where the CLI refuses IA-DIST-PATH-UNSAFE); 2, copy the workspace's .ia folder into it, leaving out .ia/work, .git and node_modules, plus any adopted source roots .ia/workspace.json binds, at their relative paths, keeping bytes and pins unchanged; 3, drop the probe file into <copy>/.ia/src/systems/<system>/probes/, or copy a drafted system folder to <copy>/.ia/src/systems/<name>/, replacing each placeholder (such as PROBE_TARGET_SYSTEM or PROBE_BOUNDARY_WORKSPACE in the examples) in every probe file as --set NAME=value did, and adding any requires to the copy's system.ia; 4, if the copy has no .ia/distributions/active.json, run ia.mjs restore --apply --yes --root <copy>; 5, run ia.mjs validate --root <copy> --max-findings 100000 --json, as the probe did, with no --severity filter (--severity names the lowest severity reported, so --severity error hides the warnings a verdict needs), and check that truncated is false; 6, judge as the probe did: a probe file is refused when any error has its path; the run is refused when any error lies outside the probe files (the probe broke something else, or the workspace already had errors); report every warning per probe file, and a draft carrying IA-GRAPH-TARGET-MISSING is not ready; 7, lint the governance keys as the probe's --lint and field-lint.mjs did: run ia.mjs capture --root <copy> --json, read <copy>/.ia/work/snapshot/current.json, collect from each @schema record the governance fields its fields section declares (the must have or may have governance.<key> as lines), and for each probe record (or, for the governance lint, each local record under .ia/src/) whose word has a schema there, report every key under its governance section that is neither declared for its word nor one of applies, requires and severity; this reads the schemas of local words too, which ia vocabulary refuses; 8, delete the copy. Report the CLI version that ran (ia.mjs doctor --json)."
    answers "What does the probe do, what do its exit codes mean, and how is a probe run by hand from this kit?"
  governance
    severity advisory
    requires "Probe only in a fresh temporary copy outside the workspace and outside ~/.ia, with every draft in one run, read every warning (never filter by severity), treat an error outside the probe files as refused, and report an environment failure as unknown, never as refused or admitted."
  subject
    subject-word check
